Conditional Longevity is an industrial design project that delves into the importance of object longevity in a consumer culture. Through the medium of umbrellas, the project explores various approaches to extending or shortening the lifespan of physical items that we use daily. The project presents three ways of designing an umberalla. Each umbrella is unique, and users are presented with impact data, benefits, and downsides of product design strategies.

The project emphasizes sustainability by focusing on reparability and using recyclable materials. The Recyclable Umbrella, for instance, is made from pure and undyed plastic, which has a high recycling rate, is lightweight, and features components that are easily replaceable. The aim is to shift responsibility to the user to maintain products, creating a healthier relationship with our material world.

Finally, the project also emphasizes the importance of high-quality materials in creating durable products. The third umbrella in the series uses ultra-high-performance materials, such as carbon fiber and stainless steel, with parts that are CNC-milled and fixed or bonded together for longevity.

Overall, Conditional Longevity serves as a thought-provoking exploration of how sustainable design principles can be incorporated into everyday objects.